kes nasty remarks about colleagues.\n\n- C: can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not very willing to further his education. Also keeps calm in a crisis, understands complicated technology.\n\n- D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Also regarded as a know-it-all, has a hot temper.\n\nWe need to discuss criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, calmness, cooperation, responsibility, etc.\n\nAgent 3 recommended B, noting communication concerns but less severe than others.\n\nWe can evaluate: B is very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical). However, makes nasty remarks about colleagues (crew cooperation, professional communication). Also weak memory for numbers (attention/accuracy). Those are significant for pilot.\n\nCandidate D: responds adequately to unexpected events (stress), concentrates well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility). But hot temper (cooperation, calmness), know-it-all (cooperation). No mention of reliability or conscientiousness. Could be less reliable.\n\nCandidate A: anticipates dangerous situations (decision quality), sees complex connections (technical), excellent spatial vision (technical), very good leadership (crew cooperation). But show-off, not open to new ideas (cooperation, adaptability). No explicit reliability or stress resilience.\n\nCandidate C: quick correct decisions (decision quality), keeps calm in crisis (stress), understands complicated technology (technical), but difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education (cooperation, adaptability). Also maybe good.\n\nWe need to decide if B still best. The hot temper of D may be severe. B's nasty remarks also severe. Which is worse? Both affect crew cooperation. B also has weak memory for numbers, which is important for attention/accuracy. D has no such memory issue. D's hot temper could affect calmness under pressure. But D's stress resilience is \"responds to unexpected events adequately\" not necessarily calm. B handles stress very well.\n\nThus B still seems strongest overall, but we might raise concerns about memory and communication. Could we propose C? C has calmness, quick decisions, technical, but egocentric and poor communication, not willing to learn. That seems worse.\n\nThus we likely agree with B, but note concerns and maybe suggest mitigation.\n\nWe need to produce a public message: state current recommendation (maybe still B), reference Agent 3's point, add our own evidence (e.g., B's weak memory for numbers, nasty remarks) and argue that despite these, B remains best, or perhaps we shift to D? Let's weigh.\n\nIf we think D's hot temper is more severe than B's nasty remarks, but D lacks explicit reliability. B is very conscientious, which is key. So B still best.\n\nThus we will agree with Agent 3, but highlight concerns and suggest that B is still the top candidate, but we need to address communication and memory issues.\n\nThus vote for Candidate B.\n\nNow produce output."}
